---
import HebrewLayout from '../../../layouts/HebrewLayout.astro';
import { PSALM119_LETTERS, DISCOVERIES, MOVEMENTS } from '../../../data/psalm119';
---

<HebrewLayout
  title="Psalm 119 — The 22 Letters — Nazaryah"
  description="A complete study of Psalm 119 through its 22 Hebrew letters. Five discoveries, the complete message, and individual letter deep-dives."
>
  <section class="hl-hero">
    <a href="/hebrew" class="hl-back">&#8592; Back to Hebrew</a>
    <div class="hero-script" dir="rtl">
      <span class="hero-paleo">𐤕𐤄𐤋𐤉𐤌</span>
      <span class="hero-modern">תהילים</span>
    </div>
    <p class="hl-label">&#8213; Psalm 119 &#8213;</p>
    <h1 class="hl-title">The Message Within the Message</h1>
    <p class="hl-sub">
      22 letters. 176 verses. Five things the psalm shows when it is read from the first letter to the last.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Tab Navigation -->
  <div class="tab-nav-wrap">
    <div class="tab-nav">
      <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="letters">The 22 Letters</button>
      <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="discoveries">Five Discoveries</button>
      <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="message">The Complete Message</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- TAB 1: 22 Letters Grid -->
  <div id="tab-letters" class="tab-content active">
    <div class="hl-section">
      <p class="tab-intro">
        Psalm 119 is an acrostic — 22 stanzas of 8 verses each, one for every letter of the Hebrew alphabet. Each letter is a door. Click any card to enter its stanza.
      </p>
      <div class="letters-grid">
        {PSALM119_LETTERS.map((letter) => (
          <article class="letter-card">
            <div class="letter-card-glyphs" dir="rtl">
              <span class="letter-card-paleo">{letter.paleo}</span>
              <span class="letter-card-modern">{letter.modern}</span>
            </div>
            <div class="letter-card-num">Letter {letter.num}</div>
            <div class="letter-card-name">{letter.name}</div>
            <div class="letter-card-picto">{letter.pictograph}</div>
            <div class="letter-card-meaning">{letter.meaning}</div>
            <div class="letter-card-verses">Verses {letter.verseStart}–{letter.verseEnd}</div>
            <a href={`/hebrew/psalm-119/${letter.slug}`} class="letter-card-enter">
              Enter &#8594;
            </a>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- TAB 2: Five Discoveries -->
  <div id="tab-discoveries" class="tab-content">
    <div class="hl-section">

      <!-- Discovery 1: Three Stretches -->
      <div class="gem-block">
        <div class="gem-label">Discovery 1</div>
        <h2 class="gem-title">The Road Has Three Stretches</h2>
        <p class="gem-subtitle">Seven letters, seven letters, then eight</p>
        <p class="tab-intro">Psalm 119 is not 176 loose sayings. Read in order, it is one man's walk with Yahuah's Law, and the walk falls into three stretches.</p>
        <div class="movements-grid">
          {MOVEMENTS.map((mv) => (
            <div class="movement-card">
              <div class="movement-num">Stretch {mv.num}</div>
              <div class="movement-range">{mv.range}</div>
              <div class="movement-verse-range">{mv.verseRange}</div>
              <div class="movement-title">{mv.title}</div>
              <div class="movement-theme">{mv.theme}</div>
              <ul class="movement-points">
                {mv.points.map((pt) => (
                  <li>{pt}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="keyline-box" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
          The Torah walk is a road with a start, a hard middle, and an end. The man does not quit the Law when the road gets hard. The hard road is where he learns it.
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Discovery 2: First / Last -->
      <div class="gem-block">
        <div class="gem-label">Discovery 2</div>
        <h2 class="gem-title">It Starts Blessed and Ends Asking to Be Found</h2>
        <p class="gem-subtitle">Verse 1 beside verse 176</p>
        <div class="firstlast-grid">
          <div class="fl-card fl-first">
            <div class="fl-tag">Aleph · Verse 1</div>
            <blockquote class="fl-quote">
              "Blessed are the undefiled in the way, who walk in the law of Yahuah."
            </blockquote>
          </div>
          <div class="fl-card fl-last">
            <div class="fl-tag">Tav · Verse 176</div>
            <blockquote class="fl-quote">
              "I have gone astray like a lost sheep; seek thy servant; for I do not forget thy commandments."
            </blockquote>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight-grid">
          <div class="insight-card">
            <div class="insight-title">What the beginning says</div>
            <p>The psalm opens with a blessing on the man who walks in the Law. It is the start of the road, and the man is full of hope: "O that my ways were directed to keep thy statutes!" (verse 5).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="insight-card">
            <div class="insight-title">What the ending says</div>
            <p>After 176 verses, the last line is not "I have arrived." It is a sheep asking the Shepherd to come find him. The man at the end knows himself better than the man at the start.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
          <div class="insight-title">What the church does with verse 176</div>
          <p>Many pulpits read this verse as proof that the Law cannot be kept — the man tried for 175 verses and failed, so the Law must be set aside. The verse says the opposite. It ends "for I do not forget thy commandments." The man who strayed is still holding the commandments. Eight verses earlier he says "I have kept thy precepts" (verse 168). Straying is not the same as throwing the Law away. The sheep that wandered is still a sheep of the flock, and it asks to be brought back to the path, not freed from it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="keyline-box">
          The walk starts with Yahuah's blessing and ends with Yahuah's seeking. In between, the servant keeps walking. Yahuah has already said what He will do: "I, even I, will both search my sheep, and seek them out" (Ezekiel 34:11).
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Discovery 3: Teaching and Walking -->
      <div class="gem-block">
        <div class="gem-label">Discovery 3</div>
        <h2 class="gem-title">Yahuah Does the Teaching, the Servant Does the Walking</h2>
        <p class="gem-subtitle">Two parts in every step</p>
        <div class="insight-grid">
          <div class="insight-card">
            <div class="insight-title">What he asks of Yahuah</div>
            <p>He says "teach me" nine times. Over and over he asks Yahuah to "quicken" him — make him alive. He asks Yahuah to open his eyes (verse 18), enlarge his heart (verse 32), and order his steps (verse 133).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="insight-card">
            <div class="insight-title">What he says he will do</div>
            <p>He will keep. He will run. He will walk. He will not forget. The two sit side by side in the same verse, again and again.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="cells-grid">
          <div class="cell-card">
            <div class="cell-title">Verse 33</div>
            <p>"Teach me, O Yahuah, the way of thy statutes; and I shall keep it unto the end."</p>
          </div>
          <div class="cell-card">
            <div class="cell-title">Verse 34</div>
            <p>"Give me understanding, and I shall keep thy law."</p>
          </div>
          <div class="cell-card">
            <div class="cell-title">Verse 32</div>
            <p>"I will run the way of thy commandments, when thou shalt enlarge my heart."</p>
          </div>
          <div class="cell-card">
            <div class="cell-title">Verse 146</div>
            <p>"Save me, and I shall keep thy testimonies."</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight-card" style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
          <div class="insight-title">What the church teaches instead</div>
          <p>One side of modern teaching says Yahuah does everything and the believer does nothing — any effort to keep the Law is "works." The other side says it is all up to the believer's choice and willpower. Psalm 119 answers both. Yahuah teaches, opens, enlarges, and gives life. The servant keeps, walks, and runs. The deliverance comes from Yahuah first, and the walk follows it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="keyline-box">
          Yahuah does not keep the Law in the man's place, and the man does not keep it in his own strength. Yahuah gives the life and the light — "thy word is a lamp unto my feet" (verse 105) — and the man walks by it.
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Discovery 4: The One Who Did Not Stray -->
      <div class="gem-block">
        <div class="gem-label">Discovery 4</div>
        <h2 class="gem-title">One Man Walked It Without Straying</h2>
        <p class="gem-subtitle">The servant of Psalm 119 and the Son</p>
        <div class="yahushua-grid">
          <div class="ycard">
            <div class="ycard-title">Every man strays</div>
            <p>Every man who has prayed Psalm 119 has had to pray its last verse too: "I have gone astray." Isaiah says the same of all of us: "All we like sheep have gone astray" (Isaiah 53:6).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ycard">
            <div class="ycard-title">One man did not</div>
            <p>One man walked the whole road of Psalm 119 and never had to pray that line. Yahushua said "I do always those things that please him" (John 8:29). Peter says He "did no sin" (1 Peter 2:22). He kept the statutes from the first letter to the last.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ycard">
            <div class="ycard-title">Why it matters</div>
            <p>Isaiah finishes the same verse: "Yahuah hath laid on him the iniquity of us all" (Isaiah 53:6). The Father provided the Lamb who never strayed, so that the sheep who did stray could be brought back. Yahushua left "an example, that ye should follow his steps" (1 Peter 2:21), and "ye were as sheep going astray; but are now returned unto the Shepherd" (1 Peter 2:25).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ycard">
            <div class="ycard-title">Not a shortcut around the Law</div>
            <p>The Son walking the road without straying does not mean the servant can stop walking it. Peter says the opposite — follow His steps. The Son's walk is the path laid down, and the servant is called back onto it.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="keyline-box" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
          A note on the first and the last: Revelation gives the title "Alpha and Omega" to Yahuah, the Almighty on the throne (Revelation 1:8; 21:6). The Son is the one who walked the road of Psalm 119 from its first letter to its last — the path the Father laid down, walked perfectly by the Son He sent.
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Discovery 5: The Whole Psalm in One Line -->
      <div class="gem-block">
        <div class="gem-label">Discovery 5</div>
        <h2 class="gem-title">The Whole Psalm in One Line</h2>
        <p class="gem-subtitle">Aleph to Tav</p>
        <div class="verdict-box">
          <p class="verdict-text">
            Yahuah seeks His servant, and the servant keeps His commandments.
          </p>
          <p class="verdict-text">
            That is Psalm 119 from Aleph to Tav. It begins with a blessing on the one who walks in the Law. It goes through affliction, straying, and tears. It ends with a servant who still holds the commandments, asking the Shepherd to come find him. The sheep does not find its own way home. The Shepherd comes. And the sheep He brings home is one who has not forgotten His commandments.
          </p>
          <p class="verdict-text">
            Revelation ends on the same note. The last blessing in Scripture is spoken over the same kind of person who is blessed in Psalm 119:1: "Blessed are they that do his commandments, that they may have right to the tree of life, and may enter in through the gates into the city" (Revelation 22:14).
          </p>
          <div class="verdict-seal">
            Psalm 119 · 176 Verses · 22 Letters · Aleph to Tav
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- TAB 3: Complete Message -->
  <div id="tab-message" class="tab-content">
    <div class="hl-section">
      <p class="tab-intro">
        In each stanza, all eight verses open with that stanza's letter, and the eight opening words make a short sentence (shown on each letter's page). Each line below draws that sentence out using the stanza's eight verses. Read together, the 22 lines tell the whole walk from Aleph to Tav.
      </p>
      <div class="complete-message-scroll">
        {PSALM119_LETTERS.map((letter) => (
          <div class="cm-row">
            <div class="cm-glyphs" dir="rtl">
              <span class="cm-paleo">{letter.paleo}</span>
              <span class="cm-modern">{letter.modern}</span>
            </div>
            <div class="cm-body">
              <div class="cm-name">{letter.name} <span class="cm-verses">· {letter.verseStart}–{letter.verseEnd}</span></div>
              <p class="cm-flow">{letter.flow}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</HebrewLayout>

<script>
  const tabBtns = document.querySelectorAll('.tab-btn');
  const tabContents = document.querySelectorAll('.tab-content');

  tabBtns.forEach((btn) => {
    btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const target = (btn as HTMLElement).dataset.tab;
      tabBtns.forEach((b) => b.classList.remove('active'));
      tabContents.forEach((t) => t.classList.remove('active'));
      btn.classList.add('active');
      const el = document.getElementById('tab-' + target);
      if (el) el.classList.add('active');
    });
  });
</script>

<style>
  /* Hero script display */
  .hero-script {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .hero-paleo {
    font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
    color: #8a6028;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .hero-modern {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.8rem);
    color: #c4a46b;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  /* Tab nav */
  .tab-nav-wrap {
    background: #120d06;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #3a2510;
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    z-index: 10;
  }
  .tab-nav {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  .tab-btn {
    background: none;
    border: none;
    border-bottom: 3px solid transparent;
    color: #6a4820;
    font-family: 'Cinzel', serif;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 1.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 1rem 1.5rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: color 0.2s, border-color 0.2s;
  }
  .tab-btn:hover { color: #c4a46b; }
  .tab-btn.active {
    color: #c4a46b;
    border-bottom-color: #BA7517;
  }

  /* Tab content */
  .tab-content { display: none; }
  .tab-content.active { display: block; }

  .tab-intro {
    max-width: 680px;
    margin: 0 auto 2.5rem;
    text-align: center;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: #c4ad84;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  /* Letters grid */
  .letters-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) { .letters-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); } }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .letters-grid { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); } }
  @media (max-width: 380px) { .letters-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

  .letter-card {
    background: linear-gradient(160deg, #1e1208 0%, #160e06 100%);
    border: 1px solid #3a2510;
    border-top: 2px solid #6a3f10;
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 1.25rem 1rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    transition: border-color 0.2s, background 0.2s;
  }
  .letter-card:hover {
    border-color: #BA7517;
    background: linear-gradient(160deg, #261608 0%, #1e1208 100%);
  }

  .letter-card-glyphs {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
  .letter-card-paleo {
    font-size: 2rem;
    color: #8a5a20;
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .letter-card-modern {
    font-size: 2.4rem;
    color: #e8c97a;
    line-height: 1;
    text-shadow: 0 0 12px rgba(232,201,122,0.25);
  }
  .letter-card-num {
    font-family: 'Cinzel', serif;
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 2px;
    color: #6a4820;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .letter-card-name {
    font-family: 'Cinzel', serif;
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #f0e0bf;
    margin: 0.15rem 0;
  }
  .letter-card-picto {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: #8a7050;
    font-style: italic;
  }
  .letter-card-meaning {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: #c4a46b;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }
  .letter-card-verses {
    font-family: 'Cinzel', serif;
    font-size: 8px;
    letter-spacing: 1px;
    color: #5a3e1a;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-top: 0.2rem;
  }
  .letter-card-enter {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-family: 'Cinzel', serif;
    font-size: 10px;
    letter-spacing: 1.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #BA7517;
    text-decoration: none;
    border: 1px solid #5a3010;
    padding: 0.3rem 0.75rem;
    border-radius: 3px;
    transition: background 0.2s, color 0.2s, border-color 0.2s;
  }
  .letter-card-enter:hover {
    background: #BA7517;
    color: #0e0d0b;
    border-color: #BA7517;
  }

  /* Gem blocks */
  .gem-block {
    margin-bottom: 4rem;
    padding-bottom: 4rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #2e1f0d;
  }
  .gem-block:last-child { border-bottom: none; margin-bottom: 0; padding-bottom: 0; }

  .gem-label {
    font-family: 'Cinzel', serif;
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 3px;
    color: #BA7517;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
  .gem-title {
    font-family: 'Cinzel', serif;
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 2.5vw, 1.9rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #f0e0bf;
    margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
    line-height: 1.25;
  }
  .gem-subtitle {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: #c4a46b;
    font-style: italic;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  /* Movements grid */
  .movements-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1.25rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 800px) { .movements-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

  .movement-card {
    background: #1a1007;
    border: 1px solid #3a2510;
    border-left: 3px solid #BA7517;
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 1.5rem 1.25rem;
  }
  .movement-num {
    font-family: 'Cinzel', serif;
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 2px;
    color: #BA7517;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }
  .movement-range {
    font-family: 'Cinzel', serif;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: #c4a46b;
    margin-bottom: 0.15rem;
  }
  .movement-verse-range {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: #6a4820;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .movement-title {
    font-family: 'Cinzel', serif;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #f0e0bf;
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
  }
  .movement-theme {
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: #c4a46b;
    font-style: italic;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #2e1f0d;
  }
  .movement-points {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .movement-points li {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: #d9c9a8;
    line-height: 1.6;
    padding: 0.35rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #1e1208;
    padding-left: 1rem;
    position: relative;
  }
  .movement-points li::before {
    content: '·';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: #BA7517;
  }
  .movement-points li:last-child { border-bottom: none; }

  /* First / Last */
  .firstlast-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .firstlast-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

  .fl-card {
    background: #1a1007;
    border: 1px solid #3a2510;
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 1.5rem;
  }
  .fl-first { border-top: 2px solid #BA7517; }
  .fl-last { border-top: 2px solid #5a3010; }

  .fl-tag {
    font-family: 'Cinzel', serif;
    font-size: 9px;
    letter-spacing: 2px;
    color: #8a6028;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .fl-quote {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: #e8d5a8;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.7;
    margin: 0;
    border-left: 3px solid #BA7517;
    padding-left: 1rem;
  }

  .insight-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .insight-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }

  .insight-card {
    background: #160e06;
    border: 1px solid #2e1f0d;
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 1.25rem;
  }
  .insight-title {
    font-family: 'Cinzel', serif;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #c4a46b;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.4rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #2e1f0d;
  }
  .insight-card p {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: #d9c9a8;
    line-height: 1.75;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .keyline-box {
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1e1208 0%, #160e06 100%);
    border: 1px solid #4a3218;
    border-left: 4px solid #BA7517;
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    color: #e8d5a8;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  /* Aleph-Tav */
  .at-ref {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1.25rem;
    background: #1a1007;
    border: 1px solid #3a2510;
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 1rem 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }
  .at-glyph {
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    color: #BA7517;
    line-height: 1;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }
  .at-ref-text {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: #c4ad84;
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .cells-grid {
    display: grid;
